Use five reduction shuffles followed by one broadcast, with a fixed mask naming physical lanes 0–18. Every caller executes all six collectives, and a lane without a partner reads itself but skips the addition.

```cpp
// sum19.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Volta or newer: exactly physical lanes 0..18 call once
// for this invocation; no other lanes call.
__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    unsigned lane;
    asm("mov.u32 %0, %%laneid;" : "=r"(lane));

    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;
    float sum = value;

    #pragma unroll
    for (int offset = 16; offset >= 1; offset >>= 1) {
        const unsigned partner = lane + static_cast<unsigned>(offset);
        const bool has_partner = partner < 19u;
        const int source = static_cast<int>(has_partner ? partner : lane);
        const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, source, 32);
        if (has_partner) {
            sum = __fadd_rn(sum, other);
        }
    }

    return __shfl_sync(mask, sum, 0, 32);
}
```

Compile this device-function translation unit with `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c sum19.cu`, or select a newer supported architecture. Call `sum19` only from physical lanes 0–18 of a full warp, with all nineteen lanes reaching this invocation once; the other thirteen lanes must not call it.

The addition order is offsets 16, 8, 4, 2, then 1. At each stage, the shuffle obtains the partner’s pre-addition accumulator, and the caller adds its own accumulator first and the partner’s second using round-to-nearest addition. The final shuffle copies lane zero’s completed result to every caller.

For exact-once coverage, initially lane i represents input i. After processing offset d, its accumulator represents precisely indices i + j*d below 19, for nonnegative integers j. Before that stage, its own accumulator covers the even j indices, while lane i+d covers the odd j indices; these sets are disjoint. If i+d is outside the range, there are no odd indices to add. Induction through d = 16, 8, 4, 2, 1 therefore gives lane zero all nineteen inputs exactly once, and the broadcast gives every caller that same result.

Every source is either a partner in 0–18 or the caller itself; the final source is lane zero. Each caller’s own bit is set, and every non-exited lane named by the mask executes the same shuffle at each stage with the same mask. These synchronized collectives supply the required rendezvous without implicit lockstep or shared-memory ordering assumptions.

Exact-once coverage describes the arithmetic expression, not exact real arithmetic: rounding is allowed, and finite inputs can still overflow intermediate float sums.

Use one shuffle to fold lanes 16–18 into logical lanes 0–2, then four butterfly shuffles to compute the sum in every caller. That uses five float shuffles per caller, with no final broadcast and no implicit lockstep assumption.

```cpp
// sum19.cu
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Contract: on Volta or newer, exactly physical lanes 0..18
// of one warp call this function once for the same invocation.
__device__ __forceinline__ float sum19(float value)
{
    unsigned lane;
    asm("mov.u32 %0, %%laneid;" : "=r"(lane));

    constexpr unsigned mask = 0x0007ffffu;
    const unsigned rank = lane < 16u ? lane : lane - 16u;

    const unsigned source = lane < 3u ? lane + 16u
                          : lane >= 16u ? lane - 16u
                          : lane;
    const float other = __shfl_sync(mask, value, source, 32);

    float sum = value;
    if (lane < 3u) {
        sum = __fadd_rn(value, other);
    } else if (lane >= 16u) {
        sum = __fadd_rn(other, value);
    }

    #pragma unroll
    for (unsigned bit = 1u; bit <= 8u; bit <<= 1) {
        const unsigned partner = rank ^ bit;
        const float peer = __shfl_sync(mask, sum, partner, 32);
        sum = (rank & bit) == 0u
            ? __fadd_rn(sum, peer)
            : __fadd_rn(peer, sum);
    }
    return sum;
}
```

Compile this device implementation with `nvcc -arch=sm_70 -c sum19.cu`, or target a newer supported architecture. Call it only from physical lanes 0–18; all nineteen must reach the same invocation, and lanes 19–31 must not call it.

Addition order: define logical leaves y0 = RN(x0 + x16), y1 = RN(x1 + x17), y2 = RN(x2 + x18), and yr = xr for ranks 3–15. The four subsequent stages combine adjacent logical groups of sizes 1, 2, 4, and 8, always adding the lower-index group first. Each addition uses `__fadd_rn`, so every caller evaluates the same rounded binary tree.

Exact-once coverage proof: the sixteen logical leaves partition the nineteen inputs into three pairs and thirteen singletons. Initially, physical lanes 16–18 duplicate the states of logical ranks 0–2, with identical operand order. After the stage with bit 2^j, each rank holds the tree sum over its aligned group of 2^(j+1) logical leaves: the XOR partner supplies the disjoint other half of that group. The duplicate lanes read partners from physical lanes 0–15 and perform the same ordered addition as their corresponding logical rank, preserving the duplication invariant. After bit 8, every caller therefore holds all sixteen logical leaves, and every original input occurs exactly once in that tree. Duplicate states are never counted as extra leaves.

Participation proof: the first shuffle reads only lanes 0–18; unpaired lanes read themselves and do not add. Every butterfly source is `rank ^ bit`, which lies in 0–15 and therefore participates. Every caller has its own bit set in the fixed mask, and all nineteen execute the same five collectives with the same mask and width, even when the first stage has no partner. The synchronized shuffles provide the required register exchange without assuming lockstep execution.

This guarantees input coverage and the specified floating-point evaluation order—not an exact real-number sum. Finite inputs can still overflow intermediate float additions, potentially producing infinities or NaNs.
